<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>org.finos.legend.pure</groupId>
    <artifactId>legend-pure-next-platform-java</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>Legend Pure - Next - Java Platform</name>

    <!--
      The Java platform, as modules. It was ONE module until the diagram extension needed a home:
      an extension's generated parser and its translated Pure do not belong in the engine's own
      `generated/`, and the only thing that keeps them apart is a module boundary.

      WHAT DEPENDS ON WHAT — the direction is the design:

          -generator                    (nothing)
          -pure                         (nothing)
          -extensions-diagram      ->   -pure
          -cli                     ->   -pure, -extensions-diagram

      The engine never references a language extension; an extension depends on the engine. That is
      what lets the diagram's translated Java compile against the engine's generated metamodel while
      living in its own tree, and it is why adding an extension cannot break the engine.

      WHY PURE IS INSIDE THE ENGINE rather than a sibling of diagram. `pure/specification/language_pure.json`
      declares `bundled: true`: `###Pure` arrives with the compiler rather than through a registered
      module, and is not optional — there is no Pure platform in which the Pure language is absent.
      The module layout says so. The CODE PATH is still uniform: both languages are `Language`
      descriptors read from their own manifests, and neither is special-cased.

      The Top grammar belongs to the engine and to no language: it splits a file into `###Section`
      blocks before any language is consulted, `###Diagram` included.

      NO SERVICE LOADER. `-cli` is the composition root and the only place the set of languages is
      named. That keeps the order explicit (`###Pure` must be installed before anything parses) and
      lets a test build a runtime with one language or two, which is what pins that an extension
      contributes nothing until its module is registered.

      LANGUAGES ARE READ, NOT GENERATED. Each module stages the manifests it owns as classpath
      resources and reads them at startup, resolving the grammar's lexer and parser by REFLECTION from
      the simple names the manifest gives. The alternative was a generator module emitting a compiled
      `Language` list, which made `DiagramParser::section` checkable by javac; reading wins on size —
      no generator module, no exec-maven-plugin, no build-time dependency — and the cost is that a
      manifest naming a grammar class or entry rule that does not exist is caught at STARTUP rather
      than by javac. Startup and not first-parse because the constructors and the rule method are
      resolved when the manifest is read; TestLanguages pins the messages that replaced the compile
      error.
    -->

    <!-- Shared by every module, so a new one compiles the same way without restating it: this is what
         the generator module was missing when it was added. -->
    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <maven.compiler.source>25</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>25</maven.compiler.target>
        <!-- Same version bootstrap and legend-pure-next-platform-truffle pin, under the same property name. -->
        <eclipsecollections.version>13.0.0</eclipsecollections.version>
        <!-- Likewise the version bootstrap pins, under bootstrap's property name. -->
        <maven.compiler.plugin.version>3.15.0</maven.compiler.plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <!--
      Eclipse Collections, managed here so every module takes the same version. It is the house
      collection library across bootstrap and Truffle, and now here.

      TODAY IT IS THE HANDWRITTEN HOST ONLY. JavaSource.compile runs javac with an EMPTY class path and
      the platform loader, so the translator's output is currently JDK-only whatever this pom says —
      that rule lives there, not here. Generated code is INTENDED to use Eclipse Collections too; when
      it does, that class path is what has to change, and these coordinates are already in one place.
    -->
    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.eclipse.collections</groupId>
                <artifactId>eclipse-collections-api</artifactId>
                <version>${eclipsecollections.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.eclipse.collections</groupId>
                <artifactId>eclipse-collections</artifactId>
                <version>${eclipsecollections.version}</version>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <!--
      Plugin VERSIONS, so the modules below configure plugins without pinning them. An unpinned plugin
      resolves to whatever version the running Maven defaults to, which makes the build depend on the
      machine; Maven says so ("'build.plugins.plugin.version' ... is missing") and a future Maven will
      refuse it outright.
    -->
    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${maven.compiler.plugin.version}</version>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
    </build>

    <modules>
        <module>legend-pure-next-platform-java-pure</module>
        <module>legend-pure-next-platform-java-extensions</module>
        <module>legend-pure-next-platform-java-interfaces</module>
    </modules>
</project>
